--! Records as values: the one thing a record needs that its syntax does not
--! give, a copy (`docs/design/ownership-checker.md` §31.15).
--!
--! A record update writes its base's own object (`language.md` §6.3): after
--! `s = { r | a = 1 }`, `r` and `s` are one record, and a later use of `r`
--! whole is a compile error. A program that keeps the old record copies it
--! first, `{ Record.copy r | a = 1 }`, and changes the copy.
--!
--! `Record` is in the prelude for qualified use only: `Record.copy` needs no
--! import, and the module takes no unqualified name from a program.

--| A shallow, fresh copy of a record: the same fields, holding the very
--| same values (`language.md` §11.12), in an object nobody else holds. A
--| change to the copy is never seen through the original, nor the other way
--| round, which is what a version kept on purpose — an undo history, a
--| snapshot — wants.
--|
--| The fields are not copied: a record of lists copies the record only.
pub foreign pure copy : { r | } → { r | }
